{0 Gaur}

Gaur is a self-contained computing toolchain built around a custom ISA,
assembly language, and virtual machine. It provides a suite of tools for
working with this ecosystem.

Gaur's primary objective is to serve as an educational tool.

{1 Terminology}

{i Coming soon...}

This will be a section on some terminology, naming conventions, etc.

{1 Tool suite}

The following tools make up the Gaur toolchain:

- {{:../assembler/index.html} Assembler}: Assembles Gaur assembly programs into Gaur binaries, or ASCII representations of Gaur binaries, or an intermediate form of assembly programs (with resolved labels and base instructions only).

- {{:../disassembler/index.html} Disassembler}: Disassembles Gaur binaries to intermediate Gaur assembly programs.

- {{:../cpu-simulator/index.html} CPU simulator}: An ISA simulator to simulate the execution of Gaur assembly programs on a Gaur processor. Shows the final processor state, the instruction-by-instruction traces and has a debug mode.

- {{:../ram-init/index.html} RAM Init}: Initializes RAM images from a textual description of the RAM. Meant to be used to generate initial RAM images to feed the CPU simulator.

- {{:../hdl-simulator/index.html} HDL simulator}: Simulating circuits described using Gaur HDL.


